N('santa-fe-depot-san-diego','Santa Fe Depot (San Diego Union Station)','San Diego','ca','rail','standing',32.71661,-117.16953,'site','public','walk','','1915–',
  'The Santa Fe Railway\'s station, opened on 7 March 1915 for visitors to the Panama-California Exposition, in place of a small wooden depot of 1887 whose clock tower was pulled down as part of the opening. San Francisco architects Bakewell and Brown designed it in Spanish Colonial Revival style. The front portico was taken off in 1954 for a car park, and Santa Fe proposed demolishing the station in 1972; it is now on the National Register and still serves Amtrak, the Coaster, the trolley and buses.',
  'The arched entrance between two towers topped by tiled domes with the Santa Fe cross. Inside the waiting room, redwood beam ceilings and walls with a coloured tile wainscot.',
  'The station San Diego built to win the transcontinental railroad, and lost to Los Angeles.',
  'A working railway station on Kettner Boulevard at Broadway, used by Amtrak, the Coaster, the trolley and buses; the waiting room is for travellers, and the front is seen from the public street. Who owns the building and the station hours were not checked.',
  [W('Santa Fe Depot (San Diego)')],
  conf='', th=None, thn=None, brick='?', hours=''),

N('california-quadrangle-balboa-park','California Building and Tower (California Quadrangle)','San Diego','ca','inst','standing',32.73153,-117.15236,'exact','open','walk','','1915–',
  'The grand entrance of the 1915 to 1916 Panama-California Exposition, designed by Bertram Goodhue and built to last, unlike most of the fair. The California Building, with its blue-and-gold tiled dome modelled on the church of Santa Prisca at Taxco, and its tower stand on the north side of the Plaza de California; Evernham Hall and the St. Francis Chapel face them. The carving mixes Gothic, Plateresque, Baroque and Churrigueresque work carried out by two brothers, Orazio and Masaniello (Tom). It was listed on the National Register in 1974 and holds the Museum of Us.',
  'The carved front and tower seen from the Cabrillo Bridge and the Plaza de California. The museum inside is open daily; tours climb the California Tower, booked through the museum.',
  'The 1915 fair\'s front door, still the way into Balboa Park.',
  'Balboa Park is city land, open at all times. The Museum of Us charges admission; hours from its own page, read November 2026. Tower tours are booked separately. Balboa Park parking has recently changed; the Spreckels Organ Society\'s page says the Lower Inspiration Point lot on Presidents Way is free for up to three hours, with a free shuttle.',
  [W('California Quadrangle'),W('Museum of Us'),['Museum of Us','https://museumofus.org/']],
  conf='', th=None, thn=None, brick='?', hours='Daily 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.'),

N('spreckels-organ-pavilion-san-diego','Spreckels Organ Pavilion','San Diego','ca','landmark','standing',32.72939,-117.15045,'exact','public','walk','','1914–',
  'An outdoor organ pavilion built for the 1915 Panama-California Exposition and given to the people of San Diego by John D. and Adolph B. Spreckels on 31 December 1914; they paid $33,500 for the organ and $66,500 for the building. With more than 5,000 pipes it is the largest pipe organ in the world in a fully outdoor setting. By the 1915 deed of gift every concert on it is free.',
  'The ornate pavilion and its curved colonnades at President\'s Way and Pan American Road East, and the organ itself on concert days.',
  'The world\'s largest outdoor pipe organ, free to hear every Sunday since 1915.',
  'In Balboa Park, city land open at all times. Free concerts on Sundays and on summer Monday evenings, from the Spreckels Organ Society\'s page (read November 2026). The same page says the Lower Inspiration Point lot on Presidents Way is free for up to three hours and park parking is free after 6 p.m.',
  [W('Spreckels Organ Pavilion'),['Spreckels Organ Society','https://spreckelsorgan.org/']],
  conf='', th=None, thn=None, brick='?', hours='Free concerts Sundays 2 p.m.; summer festival Mondays 7:30 p.m., mid-July to early September'),
